NOTICE — third-party material and EU documents
==============================================

1. EU INSTITUTIONAL DOCUMENTS
   This repository tracks EU legislative documents: the adopted AML Package acts (Regulations
   (EU) 2024/1624 and 2024/1620, Directive (EU) 2024/1640) and AMLA's Level-2/3 materials
   (public consultation papers on draft RTS/ITS/Guidelines, the Single Programming Document,
   etc.). Those documents are the work of the EU institutions, not of this repository's
   maintainer, and are NOT covered by the CC BY 4.0 licence in the LICENSE file.

   - Legislative texts of the EU are, as a rule, not subject to copyright, and the Commission's
     reuse policy (Decision 2011/833/EU) generally permits reuse of Commission documents with
     attribution. Council and Parliament documents have their own reuse terms.
   - Operative-text EXTRACTS in extracts/ are transcriptions of such documents, provided for
     tracking and diffing. They are reproduced as primary legal source material and carry the
     maintainer's annotations (which ARE under CC BY 4.0); the underlying legal wording is the
     institutions'.

2. AMLA CONSULTATION PAPERS / RESTRICTED DOCUMENTS
   The committed PDFs in sources/amla/ are AMLA's PUBLIC consultation papers and programming
   documents, downloaded from amla.europa.eu and hosted here for offline reference and diffing
   against the eventual final instruments. They are not restricted documents.

   The register (sources/README.md) records the authoritative URL for each. This repo currently
   hosts no LIMITE/restricted Council documents; should any restricted document ever be added,
   its provenance (document number, register reference, public mirror) will be recorded there.

   If you are an institutional rightsholder and have a concern about a specific file, open an
   issue and it will be addressed.

3. STAKEHOLDER MATERIAL
   Quotations from regulators, NGOs, law firms and industry are short, attributed, and used for
   identification and commentary. Copyright in those works remains with their authors.
